import{_ as t,o as d,c as o,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.yZB1U4Nl.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Limits","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"limits.md","filePath":"limits.md"}'),r={name:"limits.md"};function s(n,e,c,i,l,h){return d(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Hard caps, defaults you can raise, and what the current runtime cannot do. Every number below is a constant in the source; the file is named so you can check it.</p><h2 id="payload-and-state-caps" tabindex="-1">Payload and state caps <a class="header-anchor" href="#payload-and-state-caps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Payload and state caps&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Value</th><th>Where</th><th>Behaviour at the limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inline payload</td><td>256 KiB (262 144 bytes)</td><td><code>INLINE_LIMIT_BYTES</code> in <code>crates/types/src/lib.rs</code> and <code>src/kernel/protocol.ts</code></td><td><code>validate_event</code> rejects a larger inline <code>payload</code> with <code>payload_too_large</code>. The host offloads oversized text to the blob store and sends <code>payload_ref</code> instead.</td></tr><tr><td>Inline user state</td><td>256 KiB</td><td>same constant</td><td>The kernel sets <code>state_inline: false</code>; the host writes <code>run_state.state_ref</code> and the bytes go to the blob store. Folding then reads the blob on every event, so keep state small or <code>continue_as_new</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Inline run output</td><td>256 KiB</td><td>same constant</td><td>Offloaded to <code>runs.output_ref</code>; <code>GET /v1/runs/{id}</code> reads it back.</td></tr><tr><td>Blob reference</td><td><code>blob://sha256/&lt;64 lowercase hex&gt;</code></td><td><code>src/blob/ref.ts</code></td><td>Anything else is rejected.</td></tr><tr><td><code>random</code></td><td>exactly 32 lowercase hex chars</td><td><code>crates/kernel/src/validate.rs</code></td><td><code>invalid_event</code> otherwise.</td></tr><tr><td>Child run id</td><td><code>run_</code> + 26 hex</td><td><code>crates/kernel/src/ids.rs</code></td><td>Derived from <code>sha256(&quot;&lt;run_id&gt;:&lt;step_id&gt;&quot;)</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Numbers in hashed structures (<code>Usage</code>, <code>RetryPolicy</code>, <code>KernelState</code>) are integers. Money is <code>cost_micro_usd</code>; there are no floats on the wire where a hash is taken.</p><h2 id="size-budgets-ci-gate" tabindex="-1">Size budgets (CI gate) <a class="header-anchor" href="#size-budgets-ci-gate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Size budgets (CI gate)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>sdk/ts/sdk/scripts/size-gate.mjs</code> fails the build when any budget is exceeded, or when code that belongs to the CLI (argument parsing, doctor checks, deploy templates) appears in a chunk the runtime imports:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Artifact</th><th>Budget</th><th>Today</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>wasm/vlo_kernel_bg.wasm</code></td><td>1 MiB (1 048 576 bytes); also enforced by <code>scripts/build-kernel.sh</code> and warned on by <code>vlo doctor</code></td><td>297 573 bytes (298 KB), built with <code>opt-level = &quot;z&quot;</code>, fat LTO, <code>panic = &quot;abort&quot;</code>, <code>strip</code>, <code>wasm-opt</code> off</td></tr><tr><td>Runtime JS: every <code>dist/*.js</code> chunk reachable from <code>index.js</code> and <code>host.js</code> (what <code>@oona/vlo</code> and <code>@oona/vlo/host</code> load)</td><td>256 KB (262 144 bytes)</td><td>~207 KB</td></tr><tr><td>CLI JS: <code>cli.js</code> and the chunks only it reaches</td><td>96 KB (98 304 bytes)</td><td>~54 KB</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The CI gate on the kernel is semantic, not byte-identical: the committed wasm and a freshly built one must both pass every golden fixture (see <a href="./kernel-protocol">kernel protocol</a>).</p><h2 id="known-weak-spots" tabindex="-1">Known weak spots <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-weak-spots" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known weak spots&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These are measured, not theoretical. The setup behind the numbers is in <a href="./performance#setup">performance.md</a>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Weak spot</th><th>What was measured</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Database round trips per step</strong></td><td>One step makes about 15 round trips to the database, so step time is roughly 15 times the round trip. With a 23 ms round trip, a step took 336 ms.</td><td>Run the host in the same region as its journal.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Very wide fan-out in one run</strong></td><td>1,000 tasks inside a single run finished at 45 per second, against 975 per second across separate runs. Per-task cost grows with the width of the run.</td><td>Fan out wide work as child runs with <code>fanOutRuns</code>, each with its own journal.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Throughput is bounded by the database</strong></td><td>By design: low thousands of tasks per second on Postgres. Not load-tested on server hardware.</td><td>Add a broker as a delivery transport if you need more; the journal still records everything.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reducer purity is not enforced</strong></td><td>A TypeScript reducer could read the clock or call the network, and no lint catches it.</td><td>Keep reducers pure. A mistake surfaces as <code>nondeterminism_detected</code> when a run is rebuilt.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="dispatcher-lease-and-retry-defaults" tabindex="-1">Dispatcher, lease and retry defaults <a class="header-anchor" href="#dispatcher-lease-and-retry-defaults" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dispatcher, lease and retry defaults&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Knob</th><th>Default</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dispatcher tick</td><td>500 ms (<code>loop</code> mode only)</td><td><code>DispatcherOptions.tickMs</code></td></tr><tr><td>Claim batch</td><td>50 rows per stage per pass</td><td><code>DispatcherOptions.batch</code></td></tr><tr><td>Claim hold</td><td>30 s</td><td><code>DispatcherOptions.claimHoldMs</code></td></tr><tr><td>Refused-push backoff</td><td>1 s × 2^attempts, capped at 60 s</td><td><code>backoffBaseMs</code>, <code>maxBackoffMs</code></td></tr><tr><td>Unroutable queue backoff</td><td>60 s</td><td><code>unroutableBackoffMs</code></td></tr><tr><td>Drain time box</td><td>25 s</td><td><code>DEFAULT_DRAIN_MAX_MS</code>; <code>?max_ms=</code> on the tick route</td></tr><tr><td>Task lease</td><td>60 s</td><td><code>DEFAULT_LEASE_MS</code> (SDK), <code>DEFAULT_LEASE_TTL_MS</code> (kernel); <code>activity({ leaseMs })</code></td></tr><tr><td>Heartbeat interval</td><td><code>max(1000, lease / 3)</code> ms</td><td><code>toPushedTask</code></td></tr><tr><td>Attempt timeout</td><td>none</td><td><code>activity({ timeoutMs })</code></td></tr><tr><td>Retry policy</td><td>3 attempts, exponential, 1 s initial, 600 s max, factor 2, full jitter</td><td><code>DEFAULT_RETRY</code></td></tr><tr><td>Push request timeout</td><td>5 s</td><td><code>HttpPushTransport</code></td></tr><tr><td>Callback request timeout</td><td>10 s</td><td><code>remoteReporter</code></td></tr><tr><td>Push signature window</td><td>±5 min</td><td><code>src/host/worker/signature.ts</code></td></tr><tr><td>Signed link TTL</td><td>7 days</td><td><code>DEFAULT_LINK_TTL_MS</code></td></tr><tr><td>Poll long-wait</td><td>≤ 25 s, ≤ 10 tasks, recheck 500 ms</td><td><code>src/host/api/poll.ts</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pull worker</td><td>20 s wait, concurrency 4, ≤ 10 per poll, 1 s backoff after a failed poll</td><td><code>runPullWorker</code></td></tr><tr><td>SSE poll</td><td>500 ms; keepalive every 15 s</td><td><code>streamPollMs</code>, <code>KEEPALIVE_MS</code></td></tr><tr><td>Run list</td><td>50 via the route, 100 via <code>journal.query</code></td><td><code>indexRuns</code>, <code>DEFAULT_QUERY_LIMIT</code></td></tr><tr><td>Shutdown grace</td><td>5 s for in-process handlers</td><td><code>SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS</code></td></tr><tr><td>Default port</td><td>4100, bound to <code>127.0.0.1</code></td><td><code>DEFAULT_PORT</code>, <code>listen()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Agent loop</td><td>50 iterations, 20 observations kept</td><td><code>DEFAULT_MAX_ITERATIONS</code>, <code>DEFAULT_MEMORY_LIMIT</code> in <code>@oona/vlo/agent</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>A missed heartbeat window moves the attempt to <code>retrying</code> with <code>cause: heartbeat_lost</code>, or to <code>failed</code> once the policy is spent. <code>schema_violation</code>, <code>user_cancel</code> and <code>nondeterminism_detected</code> never retry, whatever the policy.</p><h2 id="sqlite-is-single-writer" tabindex="-1">SQLite is single-writer <a class="header-anchor" href="#sqlite-is-single-writer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;SQLite is single-writer&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The SQLite journal is one <code>node:sqlite</code> connection behind a promise mutex; every transaction takes the file&#39;s write lock with <code>BEGIN IMMEDIATE</code>. Consequences:</p><ul><li>Run <strong>one host process per SQLite file</strong>. A second process waits on the lock for <code>busy_timeout</code> (5 s) and then errors; two <code>SqliteJournal</code> instances in one process must never both be opened on the same file.</li><li>Throughput is one transaction at a time. It is the right journal for a laptop, CI and a small self-host, not for fan-out in the thousands.</li><li>Postgres has no such constraint: claims use <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> and per-run advisory locks, so several orchestrators may tick the same journal at once.</li></ul><h2 id="runtime" tabindex="-1">Runtime <a class="header-anchor" href="#runtime" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Runtime&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Node ≥ 22.5</strong> (<code>engines</code> in both packages), because the SQLite journal uses <code>node:sqlite</code> and the kernel is instantiated with <code>WebAssembly</code> from bytes read off disk.</li><li><code>zod</code> ^4 is a peer dependency of both packages; <code>@google-cloud/storage</code> ^7 is optional and only needed for <code>gcs://</code>.</li><li>The workflow bundle digest is currently <code>contentHash({ name, v: 1 })</code>: a run pins the workflow name, not its code. Changing a reducer&#39;s behaviour under a live run is caught by the re-fold check, not prevented.</li></ul><h2 id="not-available-on-edge-runtimes-yet" tabindex="-1">Not available on edge runtimes yet <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-available-on-edge-runtimes-yet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not available on edge runtimes yet&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Vlo is built to run at the edge, and it does not run there yet. The host runs on Node today. The kernel already compiles to <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> with no imports, which is what an edge host needs, but the surrounding pieces are Node-only:</p><ul><li><strong>Cloudflare Workers / Durable Objects</strong> as a host and journal are deferred. There is no Durable Object journal adapter, no <code>wrangler.toml</code> emitter, and the journal is opened with <code>pg</code> or <code>node:sqlite</code>.</li><li><strong>No cross-run index</strong> outside the <code>runs</code> table: listing and searching runs is a SQL query against Postgres or SQLite.</li><li><strong>No console.</strong> The inbox is <code>GET /v1/runs?status=awaiting_signal</code>; the timeline is the events route.</li><li><strong>No S3 or Vercel Blob adapter</strong>: blob stores are <code>file://</code> and <code>gcs://</code>.</li><li><strong>No <code>vlo.toml</code>, no native (non-Node) host</strong>: <code>crates/traits</code> sketches the adapter traits a Rust host would implement, and nothing implements them.</li><li><code>vlo deploy</code> renders templates for two targets, <code>cloud-run</code> and <code>vercel</code>, and prints the commands with <code>--apply</code>; it never runs them.</li></ul>',23)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
